From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:05:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 151/163] feat: three shipped looks, an inverse output
 transform and a space tag in every cube (S10 item 4)

The asset half of the space-aware LMT slot. Baked by tools/ocio-bake; the plugin code that
uses them is the next commit.

Every cube the tool writes now carries one machine-readable line near the top of its
provenance block:

    # ReC-LUT-Space: ACEScct

.cube has no metadata field - the Adobe/Resolve dialect is TITLE, the two sizes,
DOMAIN_MIN/MAX and comments - so the input space has to travel as a comment or not at
all. It has to travel, because a Rec.709 display LUT and an ACEScct LMT are both three
floats in [0,1] on a unit domain: the wrong one loads without complaint and produces a
wrong picture silently. Every other tool ignores the line, because it is a comment.
The four cubes that already shipped are re-baked only for this header: their sample data
is bit-identical to the previous files (verified line by line), so no picture moves.

New files:

* odt_srgb_100nit_aces2_inv_65.cube - the INVERSE of the default output transform,
  display code values in, ACEScct out. Nobody picks this; it is the second half of the
  wrap recipe. Its report measures what the inverse can and cannot recover: on the
  neutral axis the transform is invertible all the way to ACEScct 0.954 (linear ~180,
  +10 stops over grey), so almost nothing is lost to the highlight clip - what is lost is
  colour outside the display gamut, which the ACES 2.0 gamut compressor folds inwards on
  the way out.

* look_film_contrast_33.cube - soft S-curve about mid grey: Contrast 1.05, Saturation
  1.03, master curve 0 / -0.45 / 0 / +0.30 / 0 stops.
* look_warm_print_33.cube - warm shadows, cooler highlights: Lift (+0.010, +0.002,
  -0.010), Gain (0.985, 0.997, 1.015), Contrast 1.04, Saturation 0.97.

  Both are built out of NOTHING but the CineCam Grade component's own controls, in the
  chain's own order (ASC CDL -> saturation -> master curve), and each cube's header
  prints those controls back plus the single ASC CDL they fold into. Type the numbers in
  and you get the same picture, live. That is the point: the shipped looks exist to be
  readable and to prove the slot works, not to be impressive. A look worth shipping on a
  show is authored in Resolve. Deliberately absent: hue rotations, highlight
  desaturation, per-hue saturation - the things an LMT is normally for and that the
  component cannot reproduce.

  The neutral shipped look is the identity cube that already exists,
  lmt_identity_33.cube; a second 900 KB identity grid differing only in a comment would
  be waste, and it would move the "prove the plumbing" reference the docs point at.

* test_identity_rec709_33.cube - a test fixture: the identity, TAGGED as a Rec.709
  display LUT, so loading it exercises the entire wrap. The picture must not change.

Tool changes (ReC-Sandbox repo, committed separately): a ReC-LUT-Space line in
provenance_lines; kind = "inverse_odt" and kind = "look" presets; acescct.py, holding the
shaper, the ASC CDL and the monotone Hermite master curve transcribed from
CommonMath.cfi, HDRPostProcess.cfx and SceneReferredCurves.h; and wrap.py.

Research: scene-notes/research/s10-4-lmt-space-wrap.md.
Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 4.1 / 4.4.

